92186491079 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491079 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491079 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491079 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491079 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c2f04c93-2db7-56ff-bf49-28c30a11d6a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186490995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186490995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491085 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2124c99-e04c-5d5a-81fa-ec9469dfdbf9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491127 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491127 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491127 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491127 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -7)^{12},$ the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$ and the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5.$ If a basis of ${R}^{14}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491127 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "247f09ea-5442-5e08-b3bd-d68a92ad4690" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491127 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $5$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{5}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)^{4}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ must be a $14\\times 14$ matrix.\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{4}$ equals $5,$ then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=14-5=9.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ of dimension $9$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\n- Since the rank of $(A-7I)^{5}$ equals $3,$  then by the rank-nullity theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}=14-3=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to $(A-7I)^{5}v=0$ form a subspace $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ of dimension $11$ in ${R}^{14}.$\n\nSince $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}$ is a subspace of dimension $9$ in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$, which is a subspace of dimension $11,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{5}$ but do not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-7I)^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{5}-\\text{nullity}(A-7I)^{4}=11-9=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $5$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491152 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491152 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109752" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491152 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491152 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(3-\\lambda )^{2}(\\lambda -\\sqrt{2})^{15}(\\lambda -3)^{4},$ the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ equals $2,$ and the nullity of $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{21}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491152 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6337c5ef-f189-51fa-920d-63ed0153be2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491152 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $C$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2},$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ has dimension $2.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $2.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}$ is a subspace of dimension $2$ in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $2$ vectors that lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{3}-\\text{nullity}(C-\\sqrt{2}I)^{2}=4-2=2\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvectors of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\sqrt{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491198 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491198 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491198 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491198 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $C$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},$ and for the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ there is exactly $1$ eigenvector. If a basis of ${R}^{13}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $C$, how many of these generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491198 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccaaf964-e8e6-5357-a7a2-fd338a200e46" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491198 #edb/ref 1074 "The number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ that correspond to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is equal to the difference between the algebraic multiplicity and the geometric multiplicity of $\\lambda .$\n\nIn the characteristic polynomial\n\n$$\np(\\lambda )={\\lambda }^{4}(\\lambda -\\pi )^{2}(\\lambda +11)^{7},\n$$\n\nwe see that the root $\\lambda =0$ has multiplicity $4.$ So the algebraic multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $4.$\n\nHowever, there is only $1$ eigenvector corresponding to  the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0,$ so the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ is $1.$\n\nTherefore, the number of generalized eigenvectors of rank greater than $1$ corresponding to $\\lambda =0$ is $4-1=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491199 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186491017 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491199 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491199 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491199 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that a matrix $A$ has characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda )=(\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda -3)^{6}(\\lambda +2)^{3},$ the nullity of $(A-3I)^{3}$ equals $3,$ and the nullity of $(A-3I)^{4}$ equals $4.$ If a basis of ${R}^{11}$ consists of linearly independent generalized eigenvectors of $A$, how many of these are generalized eigenvectors of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491199 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d072dedc-173a-5c05-84bc-fb318762eb6c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186491199 #edb/ref 1074 "For the given matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ is a vector $v$ such that \n\n$$\n(A-3I)^{4}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-3I)^{3}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ has dimension $3.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{3}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $3.$\n\n- We are given that $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ has dimension $4.$ This means that the solutions to $(A-3I)^{4}v=0$ form a subspace of dimension $4.$\n\nNow, since $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}$ is a subspace of dimension $3$ in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$, which is a subspace of dimension $4,$ our basis can contain only $1$ vector that lies in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{4}$ but does not lie in $\\text{Null}(A-3I)^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, there will be \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{4}-\\text{nullity}(A-3I)^{3}=4-3=1\n$$\n\n generalized eigenvector of rank $4$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
